00:05:32.553 --> 00:05:37.933
Sequences past 128,000 tokens are where we pop the hood on sole text Slake,

00:05:38.313 --> 00:05:44.353
which Tech Explore reports keeps an approximate representation of discarded tokens instead of dropping them outright.

00:05:45.113 --> 00:05:47.793
Two standard approaches each fail in a different way,

00:05:48.313 --> 00:05:50.413
discarding low-value tokens loses information,

00:05:51.313 --> 00:05:55.813
while simplifying attention calculations everywhere can degrade accuracy across the board.

00:05:55.813 --> 00:06:01.333
Partially, Taylor approximated attention applies a Taylor expansion only to low value tokens

00:06:01.333 --> 00:06:06.573
and full attention on important ones, and value-aware budget-scoring ranks tokens by their

00:06:06.573 --> 00:06:08.753
expected contribution to the final output.

00:06:09.453 --> 00:06:14.153
Because the approximation retains a rough picture of the full context, information loss

00:06:14.153 --> 00:06:17.693
stays lower than pure eviction while memory usage remains fixed.

00:06:18.253 --> 00:06:22.593
The quality gain disappears once context length exceeds the point where even the retained

00:06:22.593 --> 00:06:24.953
high-value tokens no longer fit the budget.

00:06:24.953 --> 00:06:29.493
it. The regime where current large language models already strain compute and memory.

00:06:30.153 --> 00:06:35.613
Slake applies the approximation selectively and scores tokens by downstream impact, preserving

00:06:35.613 --> 00:06:41.013
the most useful signals inside a fixed memory envelope. Use it for stable long context work

00:06:41.013 --> 00:06:46.193
on hardware with strict memory caps when a small accuracy trade-off is acceptable, and fall

00:06:46.193 --> 00:06:54.113
back to standard eviction or full attention when every retained token must be exact. That's

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:58.433
useful, share it with someone who's trying to keep up with all these changes, and subscribe

